cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Helix in your research, please cite the software as below, and cite the data
  sources and models behind the results you use. Their required citations are listed in
  ATTRIBUTION.md and on the model providers page.
title: "Helix: open protein research for rare disease"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "The Helix contributors"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-03"
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Helix is an open-source platform for computational rare-disease research. It follows one path
  from disease to gene, variant, protein, structural change, mechanism, candidate interventions and
  research hypothesis, with the source and evidence class of every statement shown. The first
  dataset is the IUIS classification of human inborn errors of immunity. It is a research and
  hypothesis-generation tool, not clinical decision software.
keywords:
  - rare disease
  - inborn errors of immunity
  - protein structure
  - variant interpretation
  - provenance
  - reproducibility
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Human inborn errors of immunity: 2024 update on the classification from the International
      Union of Immunological Societies Expert Committee
    authors:
      - family-names: Poli
        given-names: M. Cecilia
      - family-names: Aksentijevich
        given-names: Ivona
      - family-names: Bousfiha
        given-names: Aziz A.
      - name: "et al."
    journal: "Journal of Human Immunity"
    volume: 1
    issue: 1
    start: e20250003
    year: 2025
    doi: 10.70962/jhi.20250003
